.TH "BASH\-SERVER\-CHANNELS" "7" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server concepts"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The v2 protocol multiplexes six logical channels over a single client
connection.
Each channel carries a distinct class of messages, allowing simultaneous
command execution, state queries, observability events, debugger
interaction, and terminal I/O without interference.
.PP
Channels are identified by a single byte (0\-5) in binary frame headers,
or by a \f[CR]\(dqchannel\(dq\f[R] integer field in NDJSON frames.
.SH CONCEPTS
.SH Channel identification
In binary wire format, the channel ID is the first byte of the 6\-byte
frame header:
.IP
.EX
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| channel  | flags | payload_length   | payload |
| (1 byte) | (1 B) | (4 bytes, BE)    | (var)   |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.PP
In NDJSON wire format, each JSON line includes a
\f[CR]\(dqchannel\(dq\f[R] field:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:1,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqeval\(dq,\(dqcommand\(dq:\(dqecho hello\(dq}
.EE
.SH Channel definitions
\f[B]CHAN_CONTROL (0)\f[R] \(en Session lifecycle
.PP
Always available on every connection.
Handles authentication, session configuration, keepalive, and graceful
disconnect.
.PP
Message types: \- \f[CR]auth\f[R] \(en Authenticate with token \-
\f[CR]auth_ok\f[R] / \f[CR]auth_fail\f[R] \(en Authentication response
\- \f[CR]ping\f[R] / \f[CR]pong\f[R] \(en Keepalive \-
\f[CR]configure\f[R] \(en Set session parameters (e.g.,
\f[CR]observe_level\f[R]) \- \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] \(en Graceful session
teardown
.PP
\f[B]CHAN_COMMAND (1)\f[R] \(en Command execution
.PP
Submits commands for evaluation and receives results.
.PP
Message types: \- \f[CR]eval\f[R] \(en Evaluate a command string; server
responds with \f[CR]stdout\f[R], \f[CR]stderr\f[R], and
\f[CR]complete\f[R] messages carrying base64\-encoded output and the
exit code \- \f[CR]execute\f[R] \(en Evaluate a pre\-parsed AST
(JSON\-serialized COMMAND tree); same response format as \f[CR]eval\f[R]
.PP
Response messages: \- \f[CR]stdout\f[R] \(en Base64\-encoded standard
output \- \f[CR]stderr\f[R] \(en Base64\-encoded standard error \-
\f[CR]complete\f[R] \(en Exit code and signal information
.PP
\f[B]CHAN_STATE (2)\f[R] \(en Shell state operations
.PP
Direct manipulation of shell internal state without executing commands.
.PP
Message types: \- \f[CR]get_var\f[R] / \f[CR]set_var\f[R] /
\f[CR]unset_var\f[R] \(en Shell variables \- \f[CR]get_func\f[R] /
\f[CR]unset_func\f[R] \(en Shell functions \- \f[CR]get_alias\f[R] /
\f[CR]set_alias\f[R] / \f[CR]unset_alias\f[R] \(en Aliases \-
\f[CR]set_trap\f[R] / \f[CR]unset_trap\f[R] \(en Signal traps \-
\f[CR]inspect\f[R] \(en List all items of a given type (variables,
functions, aliases, traps)
.PP
Each operation returns an \f[CR]ok\f[R] or \f[CR]error\f[R] response
with the requested data or error details.
.PP
\f[B]CHAN_OBSERVE (3)\f[R] \(en Observability events (server push)
.PP
Server\-initiated events that notify the client about command execution.
Must be enabled via \f[CR]configure\f[R] on CHAN_CONTROL by setting
\f[CR]observe_level\f[R] to a non\-zero value.
.PP
Event types at level 1: \- \f[CR]pre_command\f[R] \(en Emitted before
command execution; includes command string, current working directory,
line number, subshell/async flags, timestamp \- \f[CR]post_command\f[R]
\(en Emitted after command execution; includes command string, exit
status, signal number (if killed), duration in milliseconds, timestamp
.PP
Events include sequence numbers for ordering.
See \f[B]bash\-server\-observe\f[R](7) for details.
.PP
\f[B]CHAN_DEBUG (4)\f[R] \(en Interactive debugger
.PP
Breakpoint management, execution control, and AST inspection.
The debugger pauses execution when a breakpoint is hit and waits for the
client to send a resume command.
.PP
Message types: \- \f[CR]set_breakpoint\f[R] \(en Add a breakpoint
(command, line, or function pattern) \- \f[CR]delete_breakpoint\f[R]
\(en Remove a breakpoint by ID \- \f[CR]enable_breakpoint\f[R] /
\f[CR]disable_breakpoint\f[R] \(en Toggle breakpoint state \-
\f[CR]list_breakpoints\f[R] \(en Enumerate all breakpoints with hit
counts \- \f[CR]step\f[R] / \f[CR]next\f[R] / \f[CR]finish\f[R] /
\f[CR]continue\f[R] \(en Execution control \- \f[CR]inspect_ast\f[R]
\(en Serialize the pending COMMAND tree to JSON \- \f[CR]break_hit\f[R]
\(en Server\-push event when a breakpoint or step fires
.PP
See \f[B]bash\-server\-debug\f[R](7) for details.
.PP
\f[B]CHAN_PTY (5)\f[R] \(en Terminal emulation
.PP
Spawns an interactive Bash session in a pseudo\-terminal and relays I/O
between the client and the PTY.
Once a PTY session is spawned, this channel takes over the session loop.
.PP
Message types: \- \f[CR]spawn\f[R] \(en Create a PTY with specified
rows, columns, shell path, and optional ANSI stripping \-
\f[CR]input\f[R] \(en Client\-to\-PTY data (base64\-encoded) \-
\f[CR]output\f[R] \(en PTY\-to\-client data (base64\-encoded) \-
\f[CR]resize\f[R] \(en Change terminal dimensions (TIOCSWINSZ +
SIGWINCH) \- \f[CR]signal\f[R] \(en Inject a signal (SIGINT, SIGTERM,
etc.)
\- \f[CR]close\f[R] \(en Terminate the PTY session \- \f[CR]exit\f[R]
\(en Server\-push notification with child exit code
.PP
Control messages (\f[CR]ping\f[R], \f[CR]disconnect\f[R]) remain
functional during PTY mode.
See \f[B]bash\-server\-pty\f[R](7) for details.
.SH Channel availability
All channels are available after authentication.
CHAN_CONTROL is the only channel that accepts messages before
authentication (specifically, the \f[CR]auth\f[R] message).
.SH Ordering guarantees
Messages within a single channel are processed in order.
Messages on different channels may be interleaved.
Server\-push events (CHAN_OBSERVE, CHAN_DEBUG) include sequence numbers
to allow clients to reconstruct ordering relative to command responses.
